 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 9,
   "local": {"cy": "Pont Grog y Borth"},
   "text": "THE MENAI SUSPENSION BRIDGE. Thomas Telford, 1826. At 176 m, the longest span in the world when it opened, hopping a road over the strait. Be nice to it: it is nearly two hundred years old."
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "CROESO I YNYS MÔN. Welcome to Anglesey. Mam Cymru, 'Mother of Wales', because its wheat once fed the mountains. Please mind the sheep, the sheep do not mind you."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "BRYN CELLI DDU. A passage tomb 5,000 years old. On the longest day of the year the sun shines down the passage. The builders had no calendar, just a very good eye and a lot of patience."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "A LONE STONE. Nobody knows who put it here or why. The farmer says it was here when he came. The sheep say it's their rubbing post."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "LLANFAIRPWLLGWYNGYLLGOGERYCHWYRNDROBWLLLLANTYSILIOGOGOGOCH. Fifty-eight letters. The village took the name in the 1860s to attract tourists, and it worked. Locals say Llanfair PG."
  },
  {
   "x": 9,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "SORRY. The rest of the name is on the next sign. And the one after that."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 35,
   "local": {"cy": "Melin Llynnon"},
   "text": "LLYNNON MILL. One of the last working windmills in Wales. It grinds flour using only wind, grumbling, and a nice view of the sea."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 27,
   "local": {"cy": "Pont Britannia"},
   "plaque": "anglesey_britannia_bridge",
   "title": "Britannia Bridge",
   "text": "Robert Stephenson carried the railway over the Menai Strait in 1850, inside giant wrought iron tubes. In 1970 two boys exploring with a burning torch set the tar coated tubes alight and the bridge burnt out. It was rebuilt on arches, and a road deck was added on top in 1980."
  },
  {
   "x": 62,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "anglesey_red_squirrels",
   "title": "Red Squirrels",
   "text": "Anglesey is one of the best places in Wales to see red squirrels. Volunteers cleared the grey squirrels from the island, and the reds, once almost gone, have spread back through its woods. In Welsh it is the gwiwer goch. Look up, and keep quiet."
  }
 ],
